      "heading": "Working with dates / Tarihlerle çalışma",
      "segment_index": "S1.P1",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:18-18",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.tr.qmd:18-18",
      "proposition": "English states that a SEPARATE class exists which stores objects with date and time ('there is also a class that stores objects with date *and* time'); the Turkish states that the Date class itself is a class that stores date and time objects ('Bu sınıf ayrıca gün *ve* saat nesnelerini saklayan bir sınıftır').",
      "reader_consequence": "The Turkish reader concludes the Date class holds a time component, so they expect as.Date()/ymd() output to retain hours and minutes and do not understand why the separate POSIXct/datetime classes (and the ymd_hms() family taught later in the chapter) are needed at all.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Messy dates / Karışık tarihler",
      "segment_index": "S11.P1",
      "kind": "code_mismatch",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:253-261",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.tr.qmd:253-261",
      "proposition": "The Turkish prose names the parsedate function as `parse_dates()` (twice, lines 253 and 255), but the adjacent chunk and every other reference in the chapter call `parse_date()`; `parse_dates()` does not exist in the parsedate package.",
      "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader who types the function name given in the prose gets 'could not find function \"parse_dates\"' and cannot reproduce the messy-date parsing example.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Messy dates / Karışık tarihler",
      "segment_index": "S11.P1",
      "kind": "code_mismatch",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:255-261",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.tr.qmd:255-261",
      "proposition": "The Turkish prose says the example vector of character dates is \"03 Oca 2018\", \"07/03/1982\" and \"08/20/85\", but the chunk beside it parses c(\"03 Janry 18\", \"07/03/1982\", \"8-20-1985\") - two of the three inputs differ from the prose (the English prose and English chunk agree on \"03 January 2018\" / \"08/20/85\").",
      "reader_consequence": "The Turkish reader cannot match the stated inputs to the printed output, so they cannot verify which raw formats parse_date() actually accepts, which is the entire point of the example.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
